module Darklang.Cli.Docs.ForAI

let content () : String =
  """# Darklang AI Quick Reference

Darklang is a persistent package tree, not a directory of scripts. CLI process
state resets between calls; package changes persist.

## Workflow

  search --batch "List.map" "Json.serialize" --fn # related searches
  module /Darklang.Example -                     # declarations from stdin
  eval Darklang.Example.main "arg"               # test an expression
  typecheck                                       # audit declarations on the branch
  status                                          # inspect changes
  commit "message" -y                            # save them

Batch search caps each result section at three items. Use `--fn`, `--type`, or
`--val` to focus results; `--with-docs` shows full comments, `docs signatures
<module>` browses a known module, and `view <name>` shows source.

## Authoring

Create several declarations atomically:

  module /Darklang.Example - <<'EOF'
  type State = { count: Int }
  let double (n: Int) : Int = n * 2
  let main (n: Int) : Int = double n
  EOF

For one function, stdin accepts a complete declaration; the shorter signature
and body fragment remains valid:

  fn /Darklang.Example.double - <<'EOF'
  let double (n: Int) : Int = n * 2
  EOF

At module scope values use `val name = value`; `let` declares functions. Inside
a function, local values use `let`. Nested helpers need full annotations:
`let helper (x: Int) : Int = ...`.

`fn`, `type`, `val` and `module` run the at-rest type checker on what they save
and print its findings; the save goes through regardless (WIP is yours to break).
Updating existing items also re-checks their visible transitive dependents after
all propagation finishes and lists any that have definite type errors. Reports
count package locations rather than shared content hashes and do not misattribute
errors that already existed.
`commit` re-checks what it is committing and refuses a definite type error —
`commit --allow-type-errors` overrides that. An incomplete proof is reported but
never blocks: it means the checker lacked evidence, not that the declaration is
wrong. `typecheck` summarizes the whole branch; `--all` also lists successful
items, `--failed` or `--incomplete` lists only that verdict.

## Changing something that exists

Read it, transform it, write it back. Do not retype a declaration to change one
line of it:

  view Some.fn --raw > /tmp/f.dark  # the declaration alone, nothing around it
                                    # ...edit /tmp/f.dark...
  edit Some.fn /tmp/f.dark          # applies it; no editor, no terminal needed

`--raw` prints exactly what `edit` and `module` accept, so this is a pipe rather
than a screen-scrape. Plain `view` is for a person to read and carries a
capabilities line you would have to strip. `edit <name>` with no file opens
$EDITOR instead, which is the shape for a human, not for you.

## Names and syntax

- `Stdlib.X` expands to `Darklang.Stdlib.X`. Cross-owner names need a full path.
- Never write `PACKAGE.` in source or search; it is internal notation.
- Lists use commas: `[1, 2]`; chars use single quotes: `'a'`.
- `|>` passes its left value as the first argument. Parenthesize a complex left side.
- Strings concatenate with `++`. Interpolation does not coerce non-strings.
- Bare integers are arbitrary-precision `Int`; numeric operands must have one type.
- Local values use `let max = 6` without annotations; package values use `val`.
- Record construction is `State { count = 0 }`; update is `{ state with count = 1 }`.
- Option/Result cases work bare: `Some x`, `None`, `Ok x`, `Error e`.
- User enum construction uses `TypeName.Case`; match cases are bare.
- Nested constructor patterns can use `Some (Ok x)`.
- A tuple payload needs double parentheses: `Some ((a, b))`.
- A function may declare a permission ceiling as an effect row on the return
  colon: `let fetch (url: String) :{Http, Clock} String = ...`. It only
  restricts (`:{}` means effect-free); names are effect cases like `FileRead`.

Host permissions are managed with the `permissions` command (alias `perms`).
The instance policy is local, machine-wide, and deny-by-default. A fresh
installation permits package/local-store access, stdio, clock, and randomness;
rules allow (or deny) additional effects and resources:

  permissions list
  permissions allow clock
  permissions allow http GET https://api.stripe.com/v1
  permissions allow file write /srv/app/data     # roots are absolute; ~ is not expanded
  permissions deny http POST https://api.stripe.com/v1
  permissions allow native             # SQLite SQL, raw fds, process handles: cannot be
                                       # scoped, so it is granted whole or not at all
  permissions requirements <fn|hash>   # complete transitive permission requirements
  permissions show <fn|hash>           # requirements, approvals, and effective policies

Consumer approvals are local to the logged-in account on this installation.
The instance policy remains machine-wide, while each account gets its own
package approvals and logical-function pins. The normal workflow uses the
logical function name; immutable hashes stay behind the approval:

  permissions approve Darklang.Vendor.Package.function
  permissions update Darklang.Vendor.Package.function
  permissions pins                      # name  shorthash
  permissions unpin Darklang.Vendor.Package.function
  permissions approvals                 # approved hashes; revoke-package <hash> drops one

Approval computes the function's inferred transitive needs; changing the code
changes its hash and requires a new decision, without changing the user's
logical name. `unpin` only releases the name; the version's approval stays
until `revoke-package`.

## Targeted help

  docs syntax | types | operators | errors | packages
  docs stdlib | cli | scm | deprecation
  docs signatures <module>
  help <command>"""
